HUMAN-FACING FAILURE MODE, reported by Zach directly 2026-07-28: "the computer jargon copy paste is a failure mode". An agent handed him a 300-character single-line shell incantation to paste into a terminal (nested quotes, $(mktemp -d), sed range addresses, a trap, && || chaining) to identify a stray key on a remote host. His verdict: "no this is inhuman". He is right, and the existing guidance did not prevent it -- the rule an agent was following was "never ask the user to paste more than ONE LINE", which that command technically satisfied. Length is the wrong metric. The real property is whether a human can READ the thing they are about to run: a one-liner they cannot parse is worse than three lines they can, because it asks for trust without offering comprehension, on a machine where a typo can be destructive. Proposed replacement for the one-line rule: what the human pastes must be something they could plausibly have typed themselves and can eyeball for danger. If the operation genuinely needs a script, the agent SHIPS the script (scp/rsync/write it to disk) and the human runs a short named command -- `bash ~/bin/whatever.sh` -- so the complexity lives in a file that can be read at leisure, not in a paste buffer. Corollary worth stating: a human running commands under time pressure at a password prompt is the worst possible place to put unreadable code. Second, smaller lesson from the same session, same root: the agent had confined the ONLY working ssh key to a forced rrsync command, which removed its own ability to run the diagnostic, which is what pushed the work onto the human as a paste in the first place. Restricting your own access is often correct -- but do it AFTER the diagnostics, or keep a separate unrestricted path, or you convert your own work into the human do it themselves.
